# rules: csharp-certificate-check-always-passes
# languages: c_sharp
# fixture: csharp/CWE-295.cs
# from: CWE-295 (Improper Certificate Validation)
#
# A validation callback that returns true accepts every certificate: expired,
# self-signed, issued for another host, or presented by whoever is sitting in
# the middle of the connection. TLS still encrypts, so the traffic looks
# protected in a packet capture, and it is protected from everyone except the
# one attacker who matters.
#
# These are written to get past a certificate problem in development and then
# not removed, which is why the rule is worth having even though nobody would
# defend the line if asked about it. It is one of the very few findings a
# reviewer never has to think about.
#
# Two properties, because .NET has two: the process-wide
# `ServicePointManager.ServerCertificateValidationCallback`, which turns
# validation off for every connection the process makes, and
# `HttpClientHandler.ServerCertificateCustomValidationCallback`, which is
# scoped to one client. The first is worse and both are reported.
#
# The test is that the body is the constant `true` - a lambda returning it, or
# a `delegate` block whose only statement returns it. A callback that looks at
# its arguments is doing something, even if what it does is wrong, and this
# rule does not judge it: `e == SslPolicyErrors.None` in the fixture is the
# correct check and stays quiet. Judging a callback that reads its arguments
# needs to know what the check means, which a pattern cannot, and guessing
# would put a finding on every pinning implementation in the corpus.

["$O.$P = $CB;", "$P = $CB;"] as $assigned
| scan_ast("*.cs"; $assigned) as $all
| ($all | of($assigned))
| where_capture("P"; "ServerCertificate(Validation|CustomValidation)Callback$")
| focus("CB")
| where_capture("CB"; "^(\\([^)]*\\)|[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z_0-9]*)\\s*=>\\s*true$|^delegate\\s*(\\([^)]*\\))?\\s*\\{\\s*return\\s+true\\s*;\\s*\\}$")
| finding("csharp-certificate-check-always-passes";
    "this accepts every certificate - expired, self-signed, or issued for "
    + "another host - so the connection is encrypted against everyone except "
    + "whoever is in the middle of it")
| report
